WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.987 --> 00:00:03.920
- Narrator: Foxes cower at his name.

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.159
Polar bears follow his every order.

00:00:07.159 --> 00:00:10.003
The name Mr. Big strikes
fear into the heart

00:00:10.003 --> 00:00:13.115
of many a predator in Zootopia,

00:00:13.115 --> 00:00:15.172
as well it should.

00:00:15.172 --> 00:00:17.824
Mr. Big, after all, is an arctic shrew

00:00:17.824 --> 00:00:21.103
and shrews are fearsome predators.

00:00:21.103 --> 00:00:24.517
Turns out, some of the most
formidable hunters in nature

00:00:24.517 --> 00:00:26.850
come in surprising packages.

00:00:31.182 --> 00:00:33.274
Quick, think of a fearsome predator.

00:00:33.274 --> 00:00:34.309
What comes to mind?

00:00:34.309 --> 00:00:36.559
Crocodiles, cobras, tigers?

00:00:38.181 --> 00:00:39.900
You might not think to flee at the sight

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.397
of this Northern Short-tailed Shrew,

00:00:42.397 --> 00:00:44.737
and that would be a mistake.

00:00:44.737 --> 00:00:47.420
These tiny guys can take down prey over

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.489
twice their size with a single bite.

00:00:50.489 --> 00:00:51.807
Their secret?

00:00:51.807 --> 00:00:52.974
Venomous Spit.

00:00:54.049 --> 00:00:56.838
These shrews are one of
the only mammals on Earth

00:00:56.838 --> 00:00:59.081
with a toxic bite.

00:00:59.081 --> 00:01:02.569
But even they can't contend
with our next hunter.

00:01:02.569 --> 00:01:04.363
The mongoose.

00:01:04.363 --> 00:01:05.724
They mostly dine on

00:01:05.724 --> 00:01:09.585
insects, small reptiles,
birds, and rodents.

00:01:09.585 --> 00:01:11.923
But some species also go after

00:01:11.923 --> 00:01:15.423
cobras, mambas, and other venomous snakes.

00:01:16.273 --> 00:01:19.912
And, sure, it takes guts to
battle a full-hooded cobra

00:01:19.912 --> 00:01:23.134
but there's more to this
bravery than meets the eye.

00:01:23.134 --> 00:01:25.305
These mongooses are resistant to

00:01:25.305 --> 00:01:27.747
neurotoxins in snake venom.

00:01:27.747 --> 00:01:30.689
Normally, the toxins bind
to muscle cell receptors,

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:34.099
which block signals from the
brain, causing paralysis,

00:01:34.099 --> 00:01:37.629
but some mongooses have
specially-shaped receptors

00:01:37.629 --> 00:01:40.076
that fend off the toxins.

00:01:40.076 --> 00:01:42.518
Next up, the giant otter.

00:01:42.518 --> 00:01:47.068
On average, their about as
long as a human man is tall,

00:01:47.068 --> 00:01:51.310
and usually prefer prey
like catfish and piranas,

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:52.750
but on rare occasions,

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:53.910
they've been known to go after

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.043
prey at least twice their length,

00:01:56.043 --> 00:01:59.765
like anacondas and croc-like caimans.

00:01:59.765 --> 00:02:01.780
And they don't even have secret weapons,

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:06.243
like toxic saliva for help;
just their fellow otters.

00:02:06.243 --> 00:02:07.254
But believe it or not,

00:02:07.254 --> 00:02:09.291
there's another type of weasel who goes

00:02:09.291 --> 00:02:11.374
after even bigger quarry.

00:02:12.313 --> 00:02:13.819
The wolverine.

00:02:13.819 --> 00:02:17.601
Wolverines are about the
size of a border collie,

00:02:17.601 --> 00:02:20.142
and they've been known to hunt caribou.

00:02:20.142 --> 00:02:23.313
They like to ambush their prey,
and when that doesn't work,

00:02:23.313 --> 00:02:25.947
they have the gall to try and steal prey

00:02:25.947 --> 00:02:30.430
from larger predators
like wolves and bears.

00:02:30.430 --> 00:02:32.623
Last, but certainly not least,

00:02:32.623 --> 00:02:37.005
is perhaps the most unsuspecting
predator of them all.

00:02:37.005 --> 00:02:39.384
Meet the epomis beetle.

00:02:39.384 --> 00:02:40.384
Right after birth,

00:02:40.384 --> 00:02:43.975
these hungry larvae are
already on the hunt.

00:02:43.975 --> 00:02:47.816
Frogs, toads, and
salamanders are a tasty meal,

00:02:47.816 --> 00:02:51.533
but the newborns can't just
chase these large meals down.

00:02:51.533 --> 00:02:53.545
Instead, they lure their prey

00:02:53.545 --> 00:02:56.523
by tricking it into attacking them.

00:02:56.523 --> 00:02:58.098
Then, they swing around,

00:02:58.098 --> 00:03:02.583
sink their hooked jaws into
the flesh, and latch on.

00:03:02.583 --> 00:03:04.663
Mmm. Lunch time.

00:03:04.663 --> 00:03:07.531
Whether you're dealing
with a beetle or a weasel,

00:03:07.531 --> 00:03:11.278
the lesson's clear: size isn't everything.

00:03:11.278 --> 00:03:14.630
Which of these fearsome
predators surprised you the most?

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:17.810
Let us know in the comments,
and thanks for watching.

